* Inspect the deletion
Replace `path/to/file` with the tracked file to recover.
@code bash
git status --short -- path/to/file
git diff -- path/to/file
git diff --cached -- path/to/file
@end

* Deleted locally, not staged
Restore the working file from the index.
@code bash
git restore -- path/to/file
@end

* Deletion staged, not committed
Restore the file and index from HEAD. This replaces staged and local changes to this path.
@code bash
git restore --source=HEAD --staged --worktree -- path/to/file
@end

* Deletion already committed
Find a commit that still contains the file, then restore only that path.
@code bash
git log --all --oneline -- path/to/file
good_commit=abc1234 # replace with a commit containing the file
git restore --source="$good_commit" -- path/to/file
git status --short -- path/to/file
git add -- path/to/file
git diff --cached -- path/to/file
@end
If the log shows the deletion commit, its parent (`abc1234^`) may be the source you need.
Review and commit the recovered file. No branch reset is needed.

Related: {:git:}[git]. Reference: {https://git-scm.com/docs/git-restore}[git restore].
